    5 blobs, file is 81,870 bytes of ASCII
    name              torch shape          values   flat in .wts   bit-exact
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
    conv1.weight      (8, 1, 3, 3)             72           72   True
    conv1.bias        (8,)                      8            8   True
    conv2.weight      (16, 8, 3, 3)          1152         1152   True
    conv2.bias        (16,)                    16           16   True
    fc.weight         (10, 784)              7840         7840   True

    Big-endian IEEE-754 hex is a lossless representation of float32, so this is not
    'close enough' -- every value comes back bit for bit. What does NOT come back is
    the shape: `.wts` stores a flat count only, so the consumer has to know the
    layout of every blob. That is fine when the consumer is the network you are
    hand-writing anyway, and a trap the moment anyone else reads the file.
============================== End   [case_round_trip]
============================== Start [case_cost_of_ascii,(),{}]
    9,088 float32 values = 36,352 bytes raw
    format        size        vs raw   load time   keeps shape
    ------------------------------------------------------------------
    .wts (ASCII)     81,870    2.25x       3.7 ms   no
    .npz             37,640    1.04x       2.4 ms   yes

    The text format costs 2.2x the size and 2x the load time, and loses the shapes.
    It buys one thing: the file is diffable, greppable and survives being pasted into
    a bug report. For a 100 MB model that trade is a bad one -- reach for .npz or
    safetensors and keep only the idea: ship weights beside the plan, named, so a
    hand-built network can look them up.
============================== End   [case_cost_of_ascii]
