Input ->DataType.FLOAT, (4, 1, 28, 28), (4, 1, 28, 28), x
Output->DataType.FLOAT, (4, 10), (4, 10), (Unnamed Layer* 8) [Matrix Multiply]_output
    TF32 False -> max |TensorRT - torch| = 1.192e-07    (9 layers)

    Same weights, same graph, 2217x difference. **The default builder config
    enables TF32**, which rounds the mantissa to 10 bits inside the MatMul. Nothing about
    the weight transport is wrong -- with TF32 cleared the hand-built network agrees with
    torch to 1e-07, which is float32 accumulation order and nothing more.
    This is the first thing to check when a hand-built network 'does not match', and it
    is easy to misread as a transposed blob or a bad reshape.

    Two things had to be supplied by hand that ONNX would have carried: every blob's
    shape, and the fact that nn.Linear stores its weight transposed relative to what
    add_matrix_multiply wants. Both are silent if you get them wrong in a way that
    still has the right element count.
============================== End   [case_build_from_weights]
